# "What are left" when "what" (the thing that) is the subject: it takes a
# singular verb. Our own rule, from a user report; examples are ours.
# why: Here "what" means "the thing that", which is singular: what is left, what is needed. A plural verb only follows in a direct question ("What are the results?").
expr done [left, given, needed, said, done, required, missing, wanted, offered, asked, expected, decided, agreed, planned, promised, ordered, bought, sent, owed, lost, saved, found, used]
expr main <(what are @done), are>

let message "Use `is` here: `what` (the thing that) takes a singular verb."
let description "Fixes `what are left` → `what is left`."
let kind "Agreement"
let becomes "is"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Take what are left of the cake." "Take what is left of the cake."
test "Bring what are needed for the visa." "Bring what is needed for the visa."

allows "Take what is left of the cake."
allows "What are the results of the test?"
allows "Tell me what are your reasons."
